# Line endings.
#
# Before this file existed nothing normalised anything, so the ending a file got was
# whatever the editor that last touched it happened to write, and `git ls-files --eol`
# showed the result: 1921 LF against 1015 CRLF, with *every* extension split across both
# (686 CRLF `.cs` vs 33 LF, 190 CRLF `.meta` vs 1085 LF, 62 CRLF `.mdx` vs 12 LF) and two
# files -- artifacts/AvailableProfilerStats.txt, samples/.../rpg/index.js -- mixed inside
# themselves. Ten merged repos and fourteen competing `.editorconfig` files will do that;
# the configs have since been consolidated into the root one, which now says `lf`.
#
# `text=auto` is the load-bearing half, and it is the half that answers "but Visual Studio
# writes CRLF": normalisation happens on *commit*, not on save. VS, Rider and Unity can
# each write whatever their platform prefers into the working tree and the blob that gets
# committed is still LF. No editor setting, no contributor's core.autocrlf, and no
# generated-file pass can put a CRLF back into the repo while this line is here.
#
# `eol=lf` is the other half: checkouts are LF too, on Windows included. That matters
# because plenty of tooling here reads bytes rather than lines -- Biome is configured
# `lineEnding: "lf"` and Prettier defaults to it, so the repo's own formatters already
# rewrite to LF and only ever agreed with the working tree by accident (all ten CRLF
# .js/.ts files happen to sit in Biome's ignore list); docs/scripts/headingIDHelpers
# splits content on '\n' and had to be taught to carry the stray '\r' back.
#
# Nothing needs CRLF in the working tree. Unity serialises its YAML with LF on every
# platform, which is what the 1085-to-190 .meta split already reflects; MSBuild, the C#
# compiler and PowerShell are all ending-agnostic; no .sln or .csproj is tracked (Unity
# regenerates those, and .gitignore keeps them out).
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one format that genuinely misreads LF -- cmd.exe can lose
# its place scanning for a `goto` label. None are tracked today; the rule is here so the
# first one added is right rather than subtly broken.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binaries. `text=auto` already detects these by content (a NUL byte in the first 8 KiB)
# and the list below is belt-and-braces -- `binary` also switches off diffing, which is
# what you want for 423 PNG snapshots and 37 DLLs.
#
# Deliberately absent: *.asset. Unity's text serialisation makes them YAML, but
# full-sample/Assets/Scenes/Web/LightingData.asset is baked lighting data and is binary,
# so a blanket rule for the extension would be wrong for one side or the other. Content
# detection gets both right, which is the general argument for keeping this list to
# formats that are *never* text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.psd binary
*.pdf binary

*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary
*.ogg binary

*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.a binary
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary
*.aar binary
*.jar binary
*.bundle binary

*.unitypackage binary
*.pfx binary
